.\" clipt(1) -- screenshot and screen recording tool for Wayland and X11.
.TH CLIPT 1 "2026-10-31" "clipt" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
clipt \- screenshot and screen recording tool for Wayland and X11
.SH SYNOPSIS
.na
.B clipt
[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIcommand\fR] [\fIargument\fR ...]
.br
.B clipt record
[\fBscreen\fR [\fIname\fR] | \fBwindow\fR [\fIapp\fR] |
\fB\-g\fR \fIgeometry\fR | \fB\-\-stop\fR | \fB\-\-pause\fR |
\fB\-\-resume\fR | \fB\-\-toggle\-pause\fR]
.ad
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBclipt\fR freezes the screen, lets you drag a region, annotate it, and
then copy or save the result.
It is one small binary with no GUI toolkit: the overlay is drawn by clipt
itself, and captures use the compositor's own protocols.
.PP
On Wayland the capture backend is chosen automatically: ext-image-copy-capture
or wlr-screencopy on wlroots compositors, KWin's ScreenShot2 interface on KDE
Plasma, and Mutter's ScreenCast on GNOME, with xdg-desktop-portal for any
monitor that can't be captured that way and on other desktops.
On X11 it uses GetImage (RandR/Xinerama monitors) and supports INCR, so
large images paste into any application.
Every output gets its own overlay at its native resolution, so mixed
resolutions, fractional scaling and rotated monitors work.
.PP
\fBclipt record\fR records an output, a region or a window to MP4, MKV,
MOV or GIF.
On wlroots compositors it uses the capture protocols directly; on KDE Plasma
and GNOME it goes through KWin's and Mutter's screencast interfaces over
PipeWire; on X11 it reads the screen over MIT-SHM when it changes; elsewhere
it uses the ScreenCast portal, which asks once which screen to share.
Recording needs \fBffmpeg\fR.
.PP
After copying, clipt forks into the background to serve the clipboard until
something else is copied, because a Wayland clipboard is owned by a live
client.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B gui
Select a region and annotate it.
This is the default when no \fIcommand\fR is given.
The overlay opens on every output; drag a selection on any of them.
.TP
.B full
Capture the whole desktop, stitching every output into one image.
.TP
\fBscreen\fR [\fIname\fR]
Capture one output, named as shown by \fBlist\fR; the first output is used
when \fIname\fR is omitted.
.TP
.B list
Print each output's name, size, position and description, then the capture
backends available on this desktop.
.TP
.B list sound
Print the sound devices \fB\-\-audio\fR can record from: each one's name and
description, marking the ones \fBsystem\fR and \fBmic\fR stand for.
.TP
.B record
Open the selection overlay in record mode: drag an area, or click a window
or screen, then press \fBEnter\fR to start.
A red frame outside the recorded area marks it (amber while paused); the
frame is not in the video.
GNOME draws it only with clipt's extension (\fBgnome\-extension\fR).
The toolbar's speaker and microphone buttons (\fBS\fR and \fBM\fR) turn
the computer's sound and the microphone on or off for this recording; they
start from \fB\-\-audio\fR or \fBrecord_audio\fR, and other sources listed
there are kept.
The recording is saved in \fBvideo_dir\fR by default.
\fBrecord region\fR is accepted as a synonym for plain \fBrecord\fR.
.TP
\fBrecord screen\fR [\fIname\fR]
Record one output without opening the overlay, named as shown by
\fBlist\fR; the first output is used when \fIname\fR is omitted.
.TP
\fBrecord window\fR [\fIapp\fR]
Record the topmost window whose app ID is \fIapp\fR.
With no \fIapp\fR, the overlay opens so that a window can be picked; a
picked window is followed as it moves and resizes.
.TP
\fBrecord\fR \fB\-g\fR \fIgeometry\fR
Record the region \fIgeometry\fR, in slurp's "X,Y WxH" format and logical
pixels, without opening the overlay.
The region must lie within one output.
.TP
\fBrecord\fR \fB\-\-stop\fR
Stop the running recording and wait for the file to be finished.
.TP
\fBrecord\fR \fB\-\-pause\fR
Pause a running recording.
Paused time is left out of the video and the frame turns amber.
.TP
\fBrecord\fR \fB\-\-resume\fR
Resume a paused recording.
.TP
\fBrecord\fR \fB\-\-toggle\-pause\fR
Pause a running recording, or resume it if it is already paused.
.TP
.B tray
Sit in the system tray (StatusNotifierItem).
While idle, left-click captures a region and middle-click copies the whole
desktop; while recording, clicking the icon stops the recording.
The right-click menu has a 3-second-delay capture, recording controls, a
\fIRecord sound\fR checkbox (ticked when \fBrecord_audio\fR names sources;
unticked records without sound), the screenshots and recordings folders,
ticks for \fIRecord as GIF\fR, \fICopy when saving\fR and \fINotifications\fR
(\fBrecord_format\fR, \fBcopy_on_save\fR and \fBnotify\fR, saved in the config
file), \fISettings…\fR, which opens that file, an update check, and Quit.
Each capture runs as a fresh clipt process.
.TP
\fBgnome\-extension\fR
Install, for this user, the GNOME Shell extension that tells clipt where
windows are and draws the frame around a recording, and enable it; GNOME
Shell on Wayland loads it after you log out and back in, also after an
update that changes it.
GNOME gives applications no other way to do either, so without it the
overlay highlights only whole monitors there, and recordings have no frame.
The extension answers only a program named clipt.
Remove it with \fBgnome\-extensions uninstall clipt\-windows@jasondyck.github.io\fR.
.TP
\fBupdate\fR [\fB\-\-check\fR]
Check GitHub for a newer release and, for installs that clipt manages,
download it and replace the binary.
Packaged installs are pointed at their package manager.
With \fB\-\-check\fR, only report whether an update is available.
Needs \fBcurl\fR.
.TP
\fBconfig\fR [\fIkey\fR [\fIvalue\fR]]
Without arguments, list every setting with its value (as TOML).
With a \fIkey\fR, print that setting's value.
With a \fIvalue\fR as well, set it in \fIconfig.toml\fR, keeping the file's
comments and other keys; a word that isn't a TOML value counts as a string,
so \fBclipt config record_format gif\fR works.
A value the setting can't take is refused and the file left alone.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may appear before or after the command.
.TP
\fB\-o\fR, \fB\-\-output\fR \fIPATH\fR
Write the PNG to \fIPATH\fR instead of the save directory; \fB\-\fR writes
it to standard output.
With \fBrecord\fR, \fIPATH\fR is the video file and must end in \fB.mp4\fR,
\fB.mkv\fR, \fB.mov\fR or \fB.gif\fR.
.TP
\fB\-c\fR, \fB\-\-clipboard\fR
Copy the capture to the clipboard.
Applies to \fBfull\fR and \fBscreen\fR.
.TP
\fB\-g\fR, \fB\-\-geometry\fR \fIGEOM\fR
Crop a \fBfull\fR or \fBscreen\fR capture to \fIGEOM\fR in logical pixels,
in slurp's "X,Y WxH" format.
With \fBrecord\fR, records that region.
.TP
\fB\-d\fR, \fB\-\-delay\fR \fISECS\fR
Wait \fISECS\fR seconds before capturing or recording.
Fractional seconds are accepted.
.TP
\fB\-b\fR, \fB\-\-backend\fR \fINAME\fR
Force a capture backend: \fBext\fR, \fBwlr\fR, \fBkwin\fR, \fBmutter\fR,
\fBportal\fR or \fBx11\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-cursor\fR
Include the mouse pointer in the capture, where the backend supports it.
.TP
\fB\-\-fps\fR \fIN\fR
Recording frame rate, 1 to 240 (default 30); overrides \fBrecord_fps\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-gif\fR
Save the recording as a GIF, made from the recording when it stops, at most
15 frames a second.
.TP
\fB\-\-audio\fR[\fB=\fR\fISOURCES\fR]
Record sound too.
\fISOURCES\fR is \fBsystem\fR (what the computer is playing, the default),
\fBmic\fR (the default input), \fBboth\fR, \fBnone\fR, or a comma-separated
list of \fBsystem\fR, \fBmic\fR and device names from \fBclipt list sound\fR,
each optionally followed by \fB:\fR\fIPERCENT\fR, its volume in the mix
(0 to 400, default 100); for example \fBsystem:70,mic:120\fR.
Several sources are mixed into one track.
Overrides \fBrecord_audio\fR.
Needs a PulseAudio or pipewire-pulse sound server; GIFs have no sound.
.TP
\fB\-\-audio\-balance\fR \fIMODE\fR
Duck the other sound under the microphone: \fBoff\fR (the default),
\fBduck\fR or \fBsmart\fR.
With \fBduck\fR, the microphone's sound is cleaned up (high-pass,
compression, de-essing, a 3 kHz presence boost) in the mix only, and the
other sources dip while the voice speaks.
\fBsmart\fR does the same and, at the stop, says how clearly the voice
came through: its speech clarity (the Speech Intelligibility Index of its
quietest moments, 0.75 or more is good).
Below that, if turning the other sound down helps, it mixes the sound
again from what it kept as recorded, with the other sources as much
quieter as that needs (at most 20 dB), copying the video; while recording, that kept sound is a hidden FLAC file beside
the video, removed at the end.
When the microphone also hears the speakers, it instead says how far the
voice is over the rest, and leaves the mix as it is.
Needs the microphone and at least one other source; otherwise it changes
nothing and says so.
Overrides \fBrecord_audio_balance\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-separate\-audio\fR
With several sound sources, also keep each one as its own track after the
mix, at the volume it was recorded, so the balance can be changed later in an
editor.
Players play the mix.
.TP
\fB\-\-audio\-loudness\fR
Normalize the finished recording's mix to \-14 LUFS (\-1 dBTP true peak),
the loudness streaming services normalize to: the mix is measured at the
stop and then raised or lowered by one fixed gain to reach it, the video
and any separate tracks copied.
A mix already within half a LU of it is left alone.
Overrides \fBrecord_audio_loudness\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-audio\fR
Record without sound, whatever \fBrecord_audio\fR says.
.TP
\fB\-\-stop\fR, \fB\-\-pause\fR, \fB\-\-resume\fR, \fB\-\-toggle\-pause\fR
Control a running recording; see \fBrecord\fR under COMMANDS.
.TP
\fB\-\-check\fR
With \fBupdate\fR, only check for a newer release; do not install it.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Show help and exit.
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Show the version and exit.
.SH EDITOR KEYS
.TP
.B drag
Select a region.
A click selects the highlighted window or the whole screen; drag the
handles to resize the selection and drag inside it to move it.
The window or screen under the pointer is highlighted before you click;
\fBTab\fR switches between a window and its screen.
.TP
\fBV\fR, \fBR\fR, \fBE\fR, \fBA\fR, \fBL\fR, \fBP\fR, \fBM\fR, \fBT\fR, \fBX\fR, \fBN\fR
Select, rectangle, ellipse, arrow, line, pen, highlighter, text, pixelate
and numbered counter.
.TP
\fB1\fR\-\fB8\fR
Colour: red, orange, yellow, green, blue, purple, white, black, unless
\fBpalette\fR says otherwise. Right-click a colour in the toolbar to change
it with a colour wheel; \fBEnter\fR or a click elsewhere keeps the new
colour, which is saved to \fBpalette\fR, and \fBEsc\fR puts the old one
back.
.TP
\fBscroll\fR, \fB[\fR, \fB]\fR
Stroke width.
.TP
.B Shift
Constrain to squares and 45\(de lines.
.TP
.B arrows
Nudge the selection; hold \fBShift\fR to resize it.
.TP
.B V
The select tool also picks shapes: click one, then drag it or its handles.
A colour, \fB[\fR, \fB]\fR, scrolling and the arrows change the picked
shape; \fBDelete\fR removes it, and \fBEsc\fR lets go of it.
Double-click text, or click it with the text tool, to edit it.
.TP
\fBEnter\fR, \fBCtrl+C\fR, double-click
Copy the annotated image and exit.
.TP
.B Ctrl+S
Save to \fBsave_dir\fR, and copy too when \fBcopy_on_save\fR is set.
.TP
\fBCtrl+Z\fR, \fBCtrl+Shift+Z\fR
Undo and redo.
.TP
\fBEsc\fR, right-click
Cancel with \fBEsc\fR; right-click backs out a step.
.TP
.B Ctrl+R
Switch between taking a screenshot and recording a video of the selection
(record mode); the \fBScreenshot\fR / \fBVideo\fR switch at the bottom of
the screen, and the camera button in the toolbar, do the same.
In record mode \fBEnter\fR starts recording, with the sound
\fBrecord_audio\fR sets, and the annotations are hidden: a recording is of
the live screen.
.TP
\fBS\fR, \fBM\fR (record mode)
Turn the computer's sound and the microphone on or off for this recording.
.TP
\fBG\fR (record mode)
Save the recording as a GIF rather than an MP4, or back. It starts as
\fB\-\-gif\fR or \fBrecord_format\fR says, and flipping it sets
\fBrecord_format\fR for next time. A GIF has no sound.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Settings live in \fIconfig.toml\fR under \fI$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/clipt\fR
(\fI~/.config/clipt\fR when \fBXDG_CONFIG_HOME\fR is unset).
The file is TOML and every key is optional; unknown keys are an error.
A leading \fB~/\fR in a path is expanded to the home directory.
.PP
The following keys are understood.
.TP
\fBsave_dir\fR = \fI"~/Pictures/Screenshots"\fR
Where \fBCtrl+S\fR, \fBfull\fR and \fBscreen\fR save screenshots.
.TP
\fBfilename\fR = \fI"Screenshot_%Y-%m-%d_%H-%M-%S"\fR
strftime pattern for new screenshots, without the extension.
.TP
\fBcolor\fR = \fI1\fR
Starting colour, 1 to 8: red, orange, yellow, green, blue, purple, white,
black.
.TP
\fBpalette\fR = \fI["#ef4444", ...]\fR
Colours 1 to 8, as "#rrggbb". The editor's colour wheel writes this.
.TP
\fBwidth\fR = \fI4\fR
Starting stroke width in logical pixels.
.TP
\fBcopy_on_save\fR = \fItrue\fR
Saving from the editor also copies the image to the clipboard.
.TP
\fBnotify\fR = \fItrue\fR
Show a desktop notification after saving or copying.
.TP
\fBbackend\fR = \fI"auto"\fR
Capture backend: \fBauto\fR, \fBext\fR, \fBwlr\fR, \fBkwin\fR,
\fBmutter\fR, \fBportal\fR or \fBx11\fR.
.TP
\fBcursor\fR = \fIfalse\fR
Include the mouse pointer in captures, where the backend supports it.
.TP
.B font
Path to a TTF or OTF file for editor text.
When unset (the default), fontconfig's sans-serif font is used.
.TP
\fBcheck_updates\fR = \fIfalse\fR
\fBclipt tray\fR checks GitHub once a day for a newer release.
.TP
\fBvideo_dir\fR = \fI"~/Videos/Screencasts"\fR
Where recordings are saved.
.TP
\fBvideo_filename\fR = \fI"Recording_%Y-%m-%d_%H-%M-%S"\fR
strftime pattern for new recordings, without the extension.
.TP
\fBrecord_fps\fR = \fI30\fR
Recording frame rate; \fB\-\-fps\fR overrides it.
.TP
\fBrecord_codec\fR = \fI"auto"\fR
Encoder: \fBauto\fR, \fBvaapi\fR, \fBnvenc\fR or \fBx264\fR.
\fBauto\fR uses the first one that works.
.TP
\fBrecord_format\fR = \fI"mp4"\fR
\fBmp4\fR or \fBgif\fR, for recordings saved to \fBvideo_dir\fR.
An \fB\-o\fR path's extension takes precedence.
.TP
\fBrecord_audio\fR = \fI"none"\fR
Sound to record, as \fB\-\-audio\fR takes it: \fBnone\fR, \fBsystem\fR,
\fBmic\fR, \fBboth\fR or a list such as \fB"system:70,mic:120"\fR.
.TP
\fBrecord_audio_balance\fR = \fI"off"\fR
\fBoff\fR, \fBduck\fR or \fBsmart\fR, like \fB\-\-audio\-balance\fR: duck
the other sound under the microphone.
.TP
\fBrecord_separate_audio\fR = \fIfalse\fR
Also keep each sound source as its own track, like \fB\-\-separate\-audio\fR.
.TP
\fBrecord_audio_loudness\fR = \fIfalse\fR
Normalize the finished recording's mix to \-14 LUFS, like
\fB\-\-audio\-loudness\fR.
.TP
\fBmax_duration\fR = \fI3600\fR
A recording stops by itself after this many seconds; 0 means no limit.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CLIPT_DEBUG
When set (for example \fBCLIPT_DEBUG=1\fR), clipt prints startup timings
and extra diagnostics to standard error.
Bug reports are most useful with this output.
.SH FILES
.TP
\fI$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/clipt/config.toml\fR
Configuration file.
Defaults to \fI~/.config/clipt/config.toml\fR when \fBXDG_CONFIG_HOME\fR is
unset.
.TP
\fI~/Pictures/Screenshots\fR
Default \fBsave_dir\fR for screenshots.
.TP
\fI~/Videos/Screencasts\fR
Default \fBvideo_dir\fR for recordings.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, including a cancelled selection and \fB\-\-help\fR or
\fB\-\-version\fR.
.TP
.B 1
An error occurred; clipt prints a message to standard error.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ffmpeg (1),
.BR slurp (1)
.PP
